ce:section.ce:para:
Sequences were retrieved from the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) non-redundant (nr) protein sequence database using PSI-BLAST (parameters: E-value of 1.00E-30, identity > 40%, maximum subject sequence length of 500 aa) and IsPETase (NCBI Reference Sequence: WP_054022242.1) to query the model. The sequences of PETase candidates were selected from the dataset. Multiple sequence alignments were performed using the ClustalW software. Phylogenetic reconstruction was performed using maximum likelihood (mL) using MEGA11. The tree with the highest log likelihood (−8876.068) is shown. Initial trees for heuristic search were obtained automatically by applying the neighbor-joining and BioNJ algorithms to a matrix of pairwise distances estimated using the JTT model, and the topology with a superior log-likelihood value was then selected. A discrete gamma distribution was used to model the evolutionary rate differences among sites (five categories [+G, parameter = 1.09]). Bootstrap values were obtained from 1000 replicates. Sequence alignment of the enzymes used in the phylogenetic tree analysis is depicted as a graphical illustration using ESPript3 (
                        Supplementary Table S3
                        ; 
                        Supplementary Fig. S1
                        ). The pairwise identity and similarity of the proteins were calculated using the Smith-Waterman algorithm, and a sequence identity matrix was created using Excel.

All proteins used in this study were codon optimized for Escherichia coli expression. The signal peptide sequences were removed, and EcoRI and HindⅢ restriction sites were added to the 5′ and 3′ ends of the target gene sequence, respectively. The DNA sequences were synthesized by Beijing Tsingke Biotech Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China) (
                        Supplementary Tables S1 and S2
                        ). The signal peptide cleavage sites for each sequence were predicted using SignalP 6.0. Eleven candidate PETase genes, including IsPETase and LCC, were subcloned and inserted into the pET28a expression vector. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) was used to construct a recombinant plasmid to remove the signal peptides. Site-directed mutagenesis experiments were performed using PCR. The oligonucleotides used are listed in Supplementary Table S4. All the variants were verified by sequencing.

To enhance the PET hydrolyzing activity of PpPETase and ScPETase, protein structure modeling and molecular docking studies were conducted to enhance the PET-hydrolyzing activities of PpPETase and ScPETase. Structural comparisons indicated that the catalytic triad and subsite I of the substrate binding sites in PpPETase and ScPETase overlapped considerably with those in IsPETase (
                        Supplementary Fig. S4
                        ). This overlap suggests that these enzymes have similar substrate binding and catalytic activities.
